  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 204 PM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\n***Wednesday/Thursday***\n\nA longwave ridge looks to still be overhead at 500-mb Wednesday \nbased on GEFS and EC ensemble guidance. At the same time, a \nshortwave trough centered over California looks to begin moving \nanticyclonically around the ridge. A surface low at the leading edge \nof the trough is forecast to provide some showers and thunderstorms \nin the area Wednesday afternoon and evening. The best chances for \nprecipitation are across portions of Eastern Colorado, where NBM 24 \nhr precipitation guidance shows a 50-70% chance for greater than 0.1 \ninches of rain. Chances that these thunderstorms become severe \nappear marginal at this time, as LREF guidance and GFS/EC model \nsoundings show 0-6 km shear maxing out around 30 to 35 kts. The \ngreatest threat with these storms would be brief wind gusts around \n60 mph, as GFS and NAMNST soundings show the possibility of an \ninverted-V sounding profile, favorable for microbursts. As the \nshortwave trough moves east of the forecast area, chances for \nshowers on Thursday reduce dramatically. NBM precipitation guidance \nshows most of the forecast area at less than a one in three \nchance to receive greater than 0.1 inches of rain. Confidence in\nsevere weather occurring is highest on Wednesday, though is \nstill only around 5-10%. High temperatures both Wednesday and \nThursday are forecast in the 90s across the area. \n\n\n***Friday/Saturday***\n\nAs the trough begins to move southeastward across the Midwest \nFriday, a cold front may begin breaking down the upper level ridge \nto the east of the forecast area. This will cause the ridge axis to \ntranslate westward into the Four-Corners Region, and establish \napproximately north-northwesterly flow over the area at 500-mb. High \ntemperatures could hover in the lower 90s Friday and Saturday as \nthis pattern favors northeasterly surface flow. Precipitation \nchances continue to fall during this period, as PoPs (probability of \nprecipitation) fall below 15% across nearly the entire area. \n\n\n***Sunday/Monday***\n\nEnsemble guidance supports another strong trough moving across the \nNorthern United States and Southern Canada Sunday. This would once \nagain bring the ridge axis over the forecast region, but with a \npositively tilted orientation. Temperatures may rise into the mid \nand upper 90s once again Sunday and Monday as this occurs. Drier \nconditions are forecast as well, as GEFS 850-mb height mean-spread \nguidance supports approximately southwesterly surface flow under \nthis pattern. Relative humidity (RH) values may drop as low as the \nlower 20s on Sunday, and upper teens on Monday. While these RHs are \nlow, they do not meet critical fire weather criteria (<15%). \nAdditionally, NBM guidance suggests that wind gusts have less than a \n50% chance of exceeding 30 mph across most of the area either day. \nThe main exceptions are portions of Northeastern Colorado and \nSouthwest Nebraska, where there is up to a 70% chance. As such, the \ndevelopment of critical fire weather conditions across this zone may \nbe something to keep an eye on, especially since fuels are drier \nacross these areas.",
